INSERT INTO regulations (id, title, formal_title, summary, description, full_description, region, category, status, effective_date, source_name, source_url, tags) VALUES ('d4b574d8-3650-59d8-866c-d732629b8e9a', 'Regulation (EU) 2020/852 – The EU Taxonomy', 'Regulation (EU) 2020/852 – The EU Taxonomy for Sustainable Activities', 'The EU Taxonomy Regulation (2020/852) establishes a classification system for sustainable economic activities to help investors and businesses identify environmentally sustainable investments. The regulation aims to enhance capital flows into green projects, combat greenwashing, and align EU financial markets with climate and environmental goals such as the European Green Deal and the Paris Agreement.

Key provisions include:

Scope & Applicability:
Applies to financial market participants, large companies subject to non-financial reporting, and EU policymakers.
Covers financial products marketed as sustainable and corporate bonds labeled as green.
Environmental Objectives:
Climate Change Mitigation – Activities that significantly reduce greenhouse gas emissions.
Climate Change Adaptation – Investments that improve resilience to climate risks.
Sustainable Use of Water & Marine Resources – Protection of ecosystems.
Transition to a Circular Economy – Promoting resource efficiency and waste reduction.
Pollution Prevention & Control – Reducing air, water, and soil pollution.
Biodiversity & Ecosystem Protection – Preventing deforestation and habitat destruction.
Screening Criteria & “Do No Significant Harm” Principle:
Activities must substantially contribute to at least one environmental objective without significantly harming the others.
Compliance with OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises and UN Guiding Principles on Business & Human Rights is required.
Sustainable Investment & Corporate Reporting Requirements:
Large corporations must disclose the share of their revenue, CapEx, and OpEx aligned with the EU Taxonomy.
Financial products marketed as environmentally sustainable must disclose alignment with the Taxonomy.
Alignment with Other EU Regulations:
Works alongside the Sustainable Finance Disclosure Regulation (SFDR) (EU 2019/2088).
Supports EU Green Bond Standards & Corporate Sustainability Reporting Directive (CSRD).
The EU Taxonomy provides a standardized and science-based approach to defining sustainable investments, strengthening market confidence in green finance.', 'The EU Taxonomy Regulation (2020/852) establishes a classification system for sustainable economic activities to help investors and businesses identify environmentally sustainable investments. The regulation aims to enhance capital flows into green projects, combat greenwashing, and align EU financial markets with climate and environmental goals such as the European Green Deal and the Paris Agreement.

